WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.208 --> 00:00:04.417
- I don't do any email at all,
which is how I do my work

00:00:04.417 --> 00:00:05.667
when I'm actually working.

00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:08.291
The only time I do
email is when I fly,

00:00:08.291 --> 00:00:10.542
even though it's
what I'm supposed to do.

00:00:10.542 --> 00:00:14.250
Because when I'm on the ground,
that's the time to do this.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:17.500
So you're answer, and
your advantage is time.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:20.208
You need to outwork
the people that have money.

00:00:20.208 --> 00:00:25.166
There's three variables, three
pillars: time, money, talent.

00:00:25.166 --> 00:00:27.333
Talent is hard to control.

00:00:28.291 --> 00:00:30.375
Money, you don't have yet.

00:00:30.375 --> 00:00:32.500
Time is your only friend.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.750
What you do between
7 P.M. and three in the morning

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:39.583
will ultimately probably be
the variable of your success.

00:00:39.583 --> 00:00:42.083
Now if you lack talent,
you can work for the rest

00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:43.917
your life, you're not gonna win.

00:00:43.917 --> 00:00:45.417
So you need to
be smart about that.

00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:47.250
And then there's the strategy
of what you're building.

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:50.000
If you're building a
piece of shit, you've lost.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.417
But the thing that's most
controllable in sales is time.

00:00:52.417 --> 00:00:55.041
- [Man] Okay, thank you.
- You got it.

00:00:55.041 --> 00:00:56.583
- [Man 2] Hi Gary.
- How are you?

00:00:56.583 --> 00:00:57.834
- Fine, thank you.

00:00:57.834 --> 00:00:59.917
I'm one of the
co-founders of Flavor.

00:00:59.917 --> 00:01:03.125
Flavor is an online platform
where everyone between everyone

00:01:03.125 --> 00:01:04.959
can sell homemade meals online

00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:06.125
to other people
in the neighborhood.

00:01:06.125 --> 00:01:09.542
- Interesting, there was
a company that I looked at

00:01:09.542 --> 00:01:12.417
several years ago
that had a similar concept.

00:01:12.417 --> 00:01:14.291
And I love the theory, go ahead.

00:01:14.291 --> 00:01:16.083
- [Man 2] We're now
selling over 100 meals

00:01:16.083 --> 00:01:19.583
per day here in Belgium only,
Antwerp and the surroundings.

00:01:19.583 --> 00:01:22.417
My question, very obvious,
but how do you build

00:01:22.417 --> 00:01:25.291
a sustainable
two-sided marketplace?

00:01:25.291 --> 00:01:27.125
So meaning growing both supply

00:01:27.125 --> 00:01:28.875
and demand side
at the same time.

00:01:28.875 --> 00:01:33.542
- It's hard, it's why
Uber and Airbnb are special.

00:01:33.542 --> 00:01:36.959
You need to always
obsess about both.

00:01:36.959 --> 00:01:39.125
And the thing that I've seen
most two-way marketplaces,

00:01:39.125 --> 00:01:43.625
'cause everybody's the
Uber and Airbnb of X, right?

00:01:43.625 --> 00:01:46.959
They over-index on one side or
the other and then it breaks.

00:01:46.959 --> 00:01:50.708
The tension of making
both great is all you've got.

00:01:50.708 --> 00:01:54.250
So you have to literally,
you basically have to be a

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.708
perfect parent of two children.

00:01:56.708 --> 00:01:59.458
You actually have to
love both of them equally.

00:01:59.458 --> 00:02:02.458
So whenever you feel,
and one of the challenges of

00:02:02.458 --> 00:02:05.875
loving your children equally
is they live their lives.

00:02:05.875 --> 00:02:09.708
And when one's seven
and one's six, maybe that

00:02:09.708 --> 00:02:11.542
six year old is having
a tough time and you're

00:02:11.542 --> 00:02:14.500
spending 80% there, because
the seven year old's okay.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.959
But you better not spend
too much time, because then

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:17.917
if you don't spend too much
time on the seven year old,

00:02:17.917 --> 00:02:18.959
it might crack.

00:02:18.959 --> 00:02:20.667
And that's a
two-way marketplace.

00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:24.250
You have to figure out
what the health of both are,

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.917
and you have to
equally have, from strategy,

00:02:30.041 --> 00:02:32.333
real strategy against both,

00:02:32.333 --> 00:02:34.917
and then you have to
adjust on a day-to-day basis

00:02:34.917 --> 00:02:36.875
if one is underperforming.

00:02:36.875 --> 00:02:38.500
- [Man 2] And probably
start fairly local

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.166
and see how it goes there?

00:02:40.166 --> 00:02:42.917
- Yeah, nobody's ever
won two-way marketplaces

00:02:42.917 --> 00:02:44.792
by going global right away.

00:02:44.792 --> 00:02:48.708
They always lose, they raise
too much capital, it's hard.

00:02:48.708 --> 00:02:51.500
I loved the way you explained
it, like a hundred (inaudible).

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.250
I'm like, okay
good start, you know?

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.542
Now the question becomes
what's the addressable market?

00:02:55.542 --> 00:02:57.834
How big should and
could you get here?

00:02:57.834 --> 00:03:00.417
If that answer is
40,000 meals a day,

00:03:00.417 --> 00:03:02.333
you got a lot of work to do.

00:03:02.333 --> 00:03:04.375
If that answer is 1,500,

00:03:04.375 --> 00:03:06.875
not so long before you start
thinking about where else.

00:03:06.875 --> 00:03:09.500
My intuition is it's
closer to 40,000 than 1,500.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:11.417
So you got a lot of work to do.

00:03:11.417 --> 00:03:12.208
- [Man 2] Well yes, yes.

00:03:12.208 --> 00:03:13.166
- Yeah.
(audience laughter)

00:03:13.166 --> 00:03:15.875
But it's about
keeping the eye on the ball.

00:03:15.875 --> 00:03:17.500
And about caring
about both sides,

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:21.542
and by doing, back to time,
unscalable things.

00:03:21.542 --> 00:03:24.375
You know everybody wants to
find that perfect ad product

00:03:24.375 --> 00:03:26.792
that gets awareness,
but they don't want to go to

00:03:26.792 --> 00:03:29.333
the farmer's market
and shake people's hand,

00:03:29.333 --> 00:03:32.625
or like, tried-and-true
things always work.

00:03:32.625 --> 00:03:35.208
Like you still
have eight good friends

00:03:35.208 --> 00:03:37.417
and family members who
don't use your product.

00:03:37.417 --> 00:03:38.500
- [Man 2] Probably yeah.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.291
- That's insane, fix that.
- [Man 2] Okay.

00:03:41.291 --> 00:03:43.291
- You should have
108 people every day.

00:03:43.291 --> 00:03:44.458
Do you know what I mean though?

00:03:44.458 --> 00:03:45.166
- [Man 2] I'll start
calling them.

00:03:45.166 --> 00:03:47.041
- But you know what I mean?
That's important.

00:03:47.041 --> 00:03:48.834
That's important, I think
people forget, everybody's

00:03:48.834 --> 00:03:50.250
thinking about scale,
and they're not looking at

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:51.625
what's in front of them.

00:03:51.625 --> 00:03:53.750
Like, every single
person you went to school with,

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:57.542
every acquaintance,
like everybody.

00:03:57.542 --> 00:04:00.834
How many people are using it
every day, raise your hand?

00:04:00.834 --> 00:04:01.834
(group laughter)

00:04:01.834 --> 00:04:04.959
How many people have used it?

00:04:04.959 --> 00:04:06.542
You got work to do.

00:04:06.542 --> 00:04:08.375
I look at that as awesome.

00:04:08.375 --> 00:04:11.333
Like that's fun, that means
you have a lot of opportunity.

00:04:11.333 --> 00:04:12.500
- [Man 2] It is.
- You know what I mean?

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:13.959
- [Man 2] Yeah.
- Cool.

00:04:13.959 --> 00:04:16.000
- [Man 2] Okay, thanks.
- You're welcome.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:16.625
- [Yannick] Hello.
- Hey.

00:04:16.625 --> 00:04:19.000
- [Yannick] I am Yannick
from Seal Jobs.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:22.125
- How are you?
- [Yannick] Good, thank you.

00:04:22.125 --> 00:04:23.542
And I have a more
personal question.

00:04:23.542 --> 00:04:24.375
- Good.

00:04:24.375 --> 00:04:26.583
- [Yannick] How would you
describe the best

00:04:26.583 --> 00:04:30.333
possible startup
CEO/cofounder?

00:04:30.333 --> 00:04:35.291
- That's interesting,
so I'm big on sales.

00:04:35.291 --> 00:04:38.250
In general, for me
personally, and this is not a

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:40.667
tried-and-true answer, for me.

00:04:40.667 --> 00:04:43.458
I love people that have
sold stuff in their life.

00:04:43.458 --> 00:04:45.708
There's something consistent
about it that's really

00:04:45.708 --> 00:04:48.417
worked out for me
in the last 20 years.

00:04:48.417 --> 00:04:51.708
And they could be
tech nerdy, you know they,

00:04:51.708 --> 00:04:54.375
the kids I liked coming
up the game with like

00:04:54.375 --> 00:04:57.458
ripped music from Napster put
on CDs and sell it in schools.

00:04:57.458 --> 00:05:00.875
So it was a little tech, it
was a little product right?

00:05:00.875 --> 00:05:02.417
I was baseball cards
and lemonade,

00:05:02.417 --> 00:05:04.250
some people
were candy.

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:06.291
So I like sales.

00:05:06.291 --> 00:05:11.375
I try to figure out as
hard core as I can,

00:05:11.375 --> 00:05:15.542
do they need to do this
because they can't breathe

00:05:15.542 --> 00:05:17.041
if they did anything else?

00:05:17.041 --> 00:05:18.792
Or are they doing it
because they think there's more

00:05:18.792 --> 00:05:21.458
money in it, or
because it's cool now?

00:05:21.458 --> 00:05:24.458
I think that 95% of the
startup founders that I meet

00:05:24.458 --> 00:05:27.291
are doing it for
the other two things.

00:05:27.291 --> 00:05:29.333
They're either doing it cause
they think the most money

00:05:29.333 --> 00:05:31.875
is there, 'cause they
get seduced by the exits

00:05:31.875 --> 00:05:33.166
they read about.

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:37.291
Or because it's now cool
to be a startup founder.

00:05:37.291 --> 00:05:41.166
So I hate the current
state of startup, hate it.

00:05:41.166 --> 00:05:42.583
I've not been investing at all

00:05:42.583 --> 00:05:44.166
for the last year and a half.

00:05:44.166 --> 00:05:46.500
Nothing, I've made a
couple big investments

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:48.333
and things that
I'm really involved in.

00:05:48.333 --> 00:05:50.208
But, the kinda stuff that
you guys are mainly doing,

00:05:50.208 --> 00:05:52.125
I'm not even taking meetings.

00:05:52.125 --> 00:05:53.959
Because I feel like
we have the greatest era

00:05:53.959 --> 00:05:56.083
of fake entreprenuership ever.

00:05:56.083 --> 00:05:57.959
In the U.S., it just
became so big and powerful

00:05:57.959 --> 00:05:59.792
and popular, everybody did it.

00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:02.708
And in other places,
it was good for the country,

00:06:02.708 --> 00:06:04.959
the region, it was
the right thing to do.

00:06:04.959 --> 00:06:07.000
Which is both true,

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.417
the problem is it brings a
lot of people into the game

00:06:09.417 --> 00:06:10.542
that aren't meant to be.

00:06:10.542 --> 00:06:12.542
So my biggest things
that I'm looking for the best

00:06:12.542 --> 00:06:14.125
entrepreneur and
founder is are they

00:06:14.125 --> 00:06:18.166
an entrepreneur and founder,
or are they acting like one?

00:06:18.166 --> 00:06:20.750
And that takes,
and that's too hard.

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:24.166
And unlike the advice I gave
downstairs about hire and fire,

00:06:24.166 --> 00:06:28.041
you can't unfire a
$100,000 investment, you know?

00:06:28.041 --> 00:06:32.875
And so, I think that I look
for people that are driven,

00:06:32.875 --> 00:06:35.166
patient and not fancy.

00:06:35.166 --> 00:06:38.375
The amount of people
that are founders of companies

00:06:38.375 --> 00:06:41.041
who go skiing on the weekend,

00:06:41.041 --> 00:06:44.333
who go to every
marketing meetup event,

00:06:44.333 --> 00:06:47.959
who take selfies with
micro-celebrities.

00:06:47.959 --> 00:06:50.250
We're in such a bullshit era.

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:54.125
Basically, club promoters have
taken over entrepreneurship.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:06:59.583
- It's the truth.

00:06:59.583 --> 00:07:01.750
And the reason there's giggles
and head nods is we know it.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:02.625
Just became cool.

00:07:02.625 --> 00:07:04.458
Do you know what a
startup tech entrepreneur

00:07:04.458 --> 00:07:06.500
looked like 10 years ago?

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:07.667
Not like this.

00:07:09.875 --> 00:07:10.708
And not meaning,

00:07:10.708 --> 00:07:11.875
I'm sure there's
some in this room.

00:07:11.875 --> 00:07:14.750
But none were attractive,

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:16.458
none were cool.

00:07:16.458 --> 00:07:19.166
It was some nerd-ass shit.

00:07:19.166 --> 00:07:20.333
Like I got in it pretty early,

00:07:20.333 --> 00:07:21.542
and I was like, "Where the,"

00:07:21.542 --> 00:07:22.667
It was like,

00:07:22.667 --> 00:07:24.000
and I was
friends with everybody.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.375
But this was like geek culture.

00:07:26.375 --> 00:07:28.458
Web 2.0 were geeks.

00:07:28.458 --> 00:07:29.708
Period, end of story.

00:07:29.708 --> 00:07:31.500
If you were a
startup founder in 2006,

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:34.417
you were a geek, geek, geek.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.208
You know what else I look for?

00:07:40.208 --> 00:07:43.667
A lot of people are
building tech companies

00:07:43.667 --> 00:07:47.083
with no tech person
in their company.

00:07:47.083 --> 00:07:48.834
They're an
entrepreneur with an idea,

00:07:48.834 --> 00:07:51.750
and they think they can
outsource the development.

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:54.083
And they're in
the tech business.

00:07:54.083 --> 00:07:56.583
So there's just a lot
of fake shit going on.

00:07:56.583 --> 00:07:57.458
Cool.

00:07:57.458 --> 00:07:58.708
- [Yannick] Thank you.
- You're welcome.

00:07:58.708 --> 00:07:59.542
Hey.
- [Lucie] Hi.

00:07:59.542 --> 00:08:00.417
- How are you?

00:08:00.417 --> 00:08:01.667
- [Lucie] Fine, thank you.
- Good.

00:08:01.667 --> 00:08:03.834
- [Lucie] I'm Lucie,
I'm from BookWidgets.

00:08:03.834 --> 00:08:05.375
We have platform for teachers,

00:08:05.375 --> 00:08:07.792
where they can
create interactive exercises

00:08:07.792 --> 00:08:09.208
for their students,

00:08:09.208 --> 00:08:10.667
to motivate them more.

00:08:10.667 --> 00:08:12.250
- Love it.

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:14.500
And the content would
be used in the classroom?

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:16.708
Or outside the classroom?
- In the classroom, or iPads.

00:08:16.708 --> 00:08:18.750
- Okay. Yep, whatever.

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:20.250
Right, go ahead.

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:21.417
- [Lucie] Well, the problem is

00:08:21.417 --> 00:08:23.500
that teachers
are very traditional.

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:24.625
- Yes.
- [Lucie] So my question is,

00:08:24.625 --> 00:08:29.792
how do you
convince traditional people

00:08:29.792 --> 00:08:32.166
to be not traditional?

00:08:32.166 --> 00:08:34.041
- You don't.

00:08:34.041 --> 00:08:35.375
You do one of two things,

00:08:35.375 --> 00:08:38.750
you either
emphatically, aggressively,

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:40.000
through your sheer will,

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:43.792
and being not politically
correct get them there,

00:08:43.792 --> 00:08:46.667
or you wait
until they get there.

00:08:46.667 --> 00:08:48.625
You can't fight the market.

00:08:48.625 --> 00:08:50.041
Now, couple of questions,

00:08:50.041 --> 00:08:52.208
and I don't know the
school system here very well,

00:08:52.208 --> 00:08:54.375
is there
anything within the system

00:08:54.375 --> 00:08:56.166
that won't let them do that?

00:08:56.166 --> 00:08:58.917
Like does the teacher
actually make the decision,

00:08:58.917 --> 00:09:01.166
or does she or he
have to play in the rules

00:09:01.166 --> 00:09:03.166
of the superintendent,
the government,

00:09:03.166 --> 00:09:05.875
is there something else that's
not letting them use it?

00:09:05.875 --> 00:09:07.417
It's not that
they don't believe,

00:09:07.417 --> 00:09:08.583
it's that they can't,

00:09:08.583 --> 00:09:12.333
because they can have an
issue if something happens.

00:09:12.333 --> 00:09:14.291
- [Lucie] There
is a little issue.

00:09:14.291 --> 00:09:17.500
Actually, a big issue
that technology in schools,

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:18.708
it's not update-to-date,

00:09:18.708 --> 00:09:20.959
not every school
has the technology.

00:09:20.959 --> 00:09:22.500
But it's getting there,

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:26.041
so we are convincing
the teachers to get,

00:09:26.041 --> 00:09:28.375
even one iPad is
enough to use it.

00:09:28.375 --> 00:09:31.834
So to convince them to
use an iPad, that's all--

00:09:31.834 --> 00:09:34.500
- Can you give them the iPad?

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:37.834
- [Lucie] That's not
our intention--

00:09:37.834 --> 00:09:40.417
- Why?

00:09:40.417 --> 00:09:41.417
- [Lucie] Just because--

00:09:41.417 --> 00:09:42.291
- You haven't
thought about it yet?

00:09:42.291 --> 00:09:43.208
- [Lucie] No.
- Okay.

00:09:43.208 --> 00:09:44.291
(audience laughter)

00:09:44.291 --> 00:09:45.000
- It's okay.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:47.375
And do you guys
have the money to do it?

00:09:47.375 --> 00:09:47.917
- [Lucie] No.

00:09:47.917 --> 00:09:49.792
- Right, so I would reach out,

00:09:49.792 --> 00:09:52.875
and so I would go home tonight,

00:09:52.875 --> 00:09:54.000
and I would send an email

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.959
to Samsung, and Apple,
and Microsoft, and Google

00:09:58.959 --> 00:10:00.959
and I would ask
one of those companies.

00:10:00.959 --> 00:10:06.208
So Windows, Microsoft's
tablet is not doing great.

00:10:06.208 --> 00:10:09.959
You might be able to
get 50 of them for free.

00:10:09.959 --> 00:10:11.417
Go ask.

00:10:11.417 --> 00:10:12.750
And then give it.

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:15.917
And now you've
eliminated one of your problems.

00:10:15.917 --> 00:10:18.125
But a lot of startups
will go out of business

00:10:18.125 --> 00:10:21.041
'cause they were too early.

00:10:21.041 --> 00:10:23.208
And so one of the naive
things that all of us do,

00:10:23.208 --> 00:10:24.166
including me,

00:10:24.166 --> 00:10:26.458
is we think it's
going to happen faster than

00:10:26.458 --> 00:10:28.208
it actually happens.

00:10:28.208 --> 00:10:29.417
So you've gotta be careful with

00:10:29.417 --> 00:10:30.959
what I heard
really quickly here,

00:10:30.959 --> 00:10:32.625
to make sure that
you're not too early.

00:10:32.625 --> 00:10:33.500
And if you are,

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:36.041
how do you adjust to stay alive,

00:10:36.041 --> 00:10:39.750
until the market comes to you.

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:41.041
You're welcome.

00:10:41.041 --> 00:10:42.375
DRock?

00:10:42.375 --> 00:10:44.041
No?
Okay, next.

